            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>1. Before Inspection: Establish a Rules Baseline and Assign Responsibilities</h2>
<p>An advertising compliance inspection should verify more than whether a campaign can go live. The business entity, account, creative, funding, and landing page must remain consistent. For the first inspection, create a register organized by country or region, industry, platform, and client. The rules baseline should cover applicable local laws, platform policies, industry restrictions, and internal company procedures. When policies change, the operations owner should update the register on the same day and notify the relevant team members.</p>
<p>Divide responsibilities across three functions: operations should inspect accounts and campaigns, the creative team should review copy and visual assets, and finance should verify funding, invoices, and balances. Sensitive industries or uncertain legal questions should be referred to legal or compliance specialists.</p>
<h3>Pre-Inspection Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confirm that the account entity matches the advertised business;</li>
<li>Check whether the business license, official website, and landing page provide consistent information;</li>
<li>Confirm that authorization documents cover the agency relationship, target regions, and validity period;</li>
<li>Verify that the advertising account, payment method, and receiving entity are compatible.</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. Daily Inspection: Account Status and Delivery Restrictions</h2>
<p>At the beginning of each workday, access the advertising platform and review account status, balance, payment methods, review notices, and active campaigns. If a campaign is rejected, an account is restricted, a payment fails, or a policy warning appears, do not resubmit the same creative immediately. Record the error message, rule reference, screenshot, and occurrence time first.</p>
<p>Follow this sequence:</p>
<ol>
<li>Review account health and policy notices for pending actions;</li>
<li>Filter campaigns rejected, removed, suspended, or receiving limited delivery during the previous 24 hours;</li>
<li>Check whether high-spend campaigns have experienced an unexpected volume or delivery decline;</li>
<li>Reconcile platform spending, account balance, and internal funding records;</li>
<li>Assign a risk level and owner to every exception.</li>
</ol>
<p>Businesses managing multiple accounts should maintain a mapping table between account IDs and responsible owners. Shared accounts without clear ownership should be avoided. Preserve logs for every login, funding transaction, balance adjustment, and permission change.</p>
<h2>3. Weekly Inspection: Creatives, Landing Pages, and Claims</h2>
<p>Each week, review priority accounts and high-spend creatives, including ad copy, images, videos, headlines, calls to action, and final destination pages. Confirm that product names, features, prices, promotional conditions, qualification numbers, and landing-page information are consistent. Industries such as finance, healthcare, supplements, education, and recruitment may also require supporting qualifications and disclaimers.</p>
<p>Use a four-step creative review: verify the target region, identify the product category, review claims and qualifications, and compare the ad with the final landing page. If a creative uses absolute wording such as guaranteed results, zero risk, or officially lowest price, pause it and request a compliance review.</p>
<p>In one anonymous tool-app case, an updated landing page retained unsupported feature claims from an earlier version, causing new ads to be rejected. The team introduced a link between creative version, landing-page version, and review screenshot. All revisions were tested on a temporary landing page before launch, while outdated pages were withdrawn. This reduced repeated rejections.</p>
<h2>4. Funding and Reconciliation: Verify Before Acting</h2>
<p>Before adding funds, confirm the currency, amount, payer, target advertising account, and expected processing time. Each internal approval request must correspond to a platform transaction ID. Do not confirm funding based only on a chat screenshot. When an account approaches the internal balance threshold, operations should submit an alert, the account owner should approve the transaction, and finance should update the balance register.</p>
<p>After delivery, compare platform spending, balance changes, and bank or payment-provider transactions daily. Consolidate account-level differences weekly. Reconcile invoices, payment entities, and service fees monthly. If duplicate charges, missing credits, or unexplained cross-account balances appear, pause the relevant transaction, preserve the billing records and payment evidence, and ask the platform or payment provider to investigate. Creating a new account to conceal an unresolved accounting issue is not an acceptable workaround.</p>
<h2>5. Exception Triage and Escalation</h2>
<p>Classify exceptions into three levels:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Low risk:</strong> Minor copy corrections, insufficient balance, or missing supporting information for one creative. The owner should resolve the issue on the same day.</li>
<li><strong>Elevated risk:</strong> Repeated rejections, inconsistent landing-page information, failed payments, or unauthorized permission changes. Pause the affected campaigns and escalate within four business hours.</li>
<li><strong>Critical risk:</strong> Account restrictions, suspected fraudulent transactions, questionable sensitive-industry qualifications, or significant funding discrepancies. Stop funding and delivery immediately and notify the compliance owner and client contact.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use a standard exception record containing the date and time, platform, account, creative ID, risk level, impact, owner, action taken, review result, and closure time. When a rule is unclear, pause the higher-risk action and gather evidence. Operations staff should not independently interpret the policy or guarantee the outcome of an appeal.</p>
<h2>6. Documentation and Review: Make Every Inspection Traceable</h2>
<p>Archive screenshots, review receipts, funding records, reconciliation files, and resolution notes under the relevant country, platform, and client. Apply the company’s retention policy and local requirements. Review common rejection reasons, balance discrepancies, and account exceptions monthly. Use the findings to update prohibited wording, landing-page standards, and operating procedures.</p>
<p>The final standard is not that every campaign passes review. It is that every account has an owner, every transaction has a matching record, and every exception has a clear status and supporting closure evidence. This turns overseas advertising compliance from emergency troubleshooting into a repeatable operating process.</p>
